  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA (descriptive, verb-disciplined) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>Oman announced on September 13 the postponement of a ministerial-level gathering in Salalah between Iran, Iraq, and all six GCC member states — the first such collective grouping planned since the February 28 war outbreak. Foreign Minister Badr Albusaidi described the delay as "in the interest of consensus"; Bahrain declined to attend, and multiple Gulf states requested deferral; Iran agreed through its MFA, attributing the delay to Gulf parties rather than its own position (Fars, Sep 13). No replacement date was scheduled. The meeting had no mandate to speak for the United States, but the collapse of even this multilateral track removes a condition that might have enabled a sequenced US-Iran approach. → moved <span class="cite">F6</span></p>

      <p>Weekend AIS data through Hormuz showed four commercial exits and ten entries on September 13, with commodity transits remaining well below the 10-day rolling average of 14 vessels per day (93.3 The Drive, Sep 13). UK Maritime Trade Operations reported a vessel struck by an unidentified projectile near Hengam Island around 0500 local on September 13, killing one crew member and wounding four; the attacker was not confirmed. The Joint Maritime Information Centre maintained its 'SEVERE' risk classification for Hormuz transits, with ongoing warnings of uncharted mines. → moved <span class="cite">F7</span>, supports <span class="cite">F4</span> without move</p>

      <p>An i24NEWS/Direct Polls survey published September 9 recorded Likud at 27 seats and Yashar at 22 — the first major poll of the 2026 Knesset campaign to give Likud a plurality. The Jerusalem Post described the overall polling landscape on September 12 as a genuine split: four of five publicly available polls show Yashar ahead by two to four seats, while the i24 poll shows Likud ahead by five. The Haaretz Channel 12 poll published September 8 had shown the reverse: Yashar 25, Likud 21. Bloc arithmetic remains uncertain across all polling firms, with none projecting either side at 61 seats. → moved <span class="cite">F5</span>, supports <span class="cite">F1</span></p>

      <p>No CENTCOM press release or confirmed reporting documented an IRGC ballistic or cruise missile attack against US military personnel or assets on September 13. The Lebanon front was quiet for a second consecutive day, with no Hezbollah fire or Israeli strike reported. The IRGC's September 9 maritime restricted-area announcement remained without coordinates, an effective date, or a designated enforcement authority as of September 14. The near-daily exchange cadence of September 1–11 produced no confirmed continuation over September 12–13, though the two-day quiet is insufficient to reduce the base-rate estimate for the September 16 horizon. → supports <span class="cite">F2</span> without move</p>

      <p>China's foreign ministry stated September 13 that Beijing would actively support Oman's mediation efforts and repeated its call for a Hormuz reopening; the statement carried no new mechanism or timetable. Trump's September 9 framing — that the war would end immediately after the November 3 US midterm elections — remained the dominant US public position, with no White House correction or modification reported in the September 13-14 window. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

